#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106651} :activity/id #uuid "eb407cdf-9aea-5548-a574-970269d4ce5b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048184 :topic/id #uuid "86927b03-ba99-5587-9673-31a5bae6ef0b" :topic/math-academy-id 1317} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107178 :tutorial/content "The shape below consists of $6$ line segments defined by the $6$ points $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$, and $F$. These line segments form a loop.\n\n![](images/25/259bfd166a7870bd1a057b28d4b3324f3b98d05222596f1426afa01d6573e3d8.png)\n\nA chain of straight line segments that form a loop is called a **polygon**.\n\nThe line segments are the **sides** of the polygon, and the endpoints of the sides are the **vertices**.\n\nFor this polygon:\n\n- The $6$ vertices are $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$, and $F$.\n- The $6$ sides are $\\overline{AB}$, $\\overline{BC}$, $\\overline{CD}$, $\\overline{DE}$, $\\overline{EF}$, and $\\overline{AF}$.\n\nTo denote a particular polygon, we start at one vertex and loop around the polygon, reading off the vertices in order. So, the polygon above can be denoted $ABCDEF$.\n\nThe singular form of the word *vertices* is **vertex**. So, $A$ is a vertex, $B$ is a vertex, etc." :tutorial/id #uuid "342100ff-c1a7-531c-90a6-e07a80e83fda" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9674 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5fbe01e1-2066-5d27-b53a-a2fa74fc43e6" :step/math-academy-id 188116 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106652}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107179 :tutorial/content "The name of a polygon is determined by the number of sides it has.\n\nThe polygon below has $6$ sides. A six-sided polygon is called a **hexagon**.\n\n![](images/25/259bfd166a7870bd1a057b28d4b3324f3b98d05222596f1426afa01d6573e3d8.png)\n\nThe names of the polygons with up to ten sides are given below:\n\n| Number of sides | Name |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $3$ | Triangle |\n| $4$ | Quadrilateral |\n| $5$ | Pentagon |\n| $6$ | Hexagon |\n| $7$ | Heptagon |\n| $8$ | Octagon |\n| $9$ | Nonagon |\n| $10$ | Decagon |" :tutorial/id #uuid "9aaf4a28-03c1-57ea-adcd-968b00ad8a41" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9675 :tutorial/title "Naming Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "d4d97dfe-283b-5019-b2e4-5d6ab6550b1d" :step/math-academy-id 188117 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106653}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105754 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105491 :question/id #uuid "3f0d9dbb-448f-5e1e-b06c-4b6a3a674b09" :question/math-academy-id "e-15616"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9fd1d3c-8479-5b35-abbc-9664cea2e1a8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049350}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106195}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "5e398f9a-e437-5d98-bf9e-cb85ba65689b" :step/math-academy-id 188118 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106654}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107182 :tutorial/content "We can categorize polygons in different ways.\n\nA polygon is **simple** if its boundary does not intersect itself. Otherwise, the polygon is **complex**.\n\n![](images/71/71ef7dbf87cb3a097ec153eb4c4e18920c2567e6a239a788f4340a0a5f33ea6f.png)\n\nA polygon is **convex** if it has no angles pointing inwards. Otherwise, if the polygon has angles pointing inwards, we call it **concave**.\n\n![](images/ef/ef7357c0e21760c3e7f159c253c527e35aac869ce16381132a53356dc53455e2.png)\n\nIt might help to remember that a con**cave** polygon looks a bit like the entrance to a **cave** when viewed from the outside.\n\n![](images/11/11e9844e49a5c586ec573e08865eb7d02dd68459e90df90749116a9eb1575430.png)\n\n**Note**: A more technical way to define a convex polygon is to say that all of its *internal angles* are less than $180^\\circ$. If you're not sure what an internal angle is yet, don't worry! We'll learn more about the internal angles of a polygon in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "b7c27ce6-fd4a-5560-bc80-0e72700ff320" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9678 :tutorial/title "Types of Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "e39203ba-5fa0-58f6-9ad6-4003cf7c5c77" :step/math-academy-id 188121 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105494 :question/id #uuid "d56604cb-e46a-5d52-94c4-dea5e8c0df3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-15623"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d25a77b-7977-50b2-b375-51b8539bc9b9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106216}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Type of a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "ac8b8e7b-3a84-5a11-ab36-35cd5a021aa5" :step/math-academy-id 188122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107180 :tutorial/content "Consider the pentagon $ABCDE$, shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b11b509e881cd813ff6879a0a6fa9559d51ffcbc5da4c150a0eaf0a9ee82a159.png)\n\nWe have the following definitions:\n\n- Two *sides* are **adjacent** (or **consecutive**) if they have a common vertex.For example, $\\color[rgb]{0.753,0.494,0.643}\\overline{BC}$ is adjacent to $\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}\\overline{AB}$ *and* ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}\\overline{CD}}$.![](images/76/760a9cbccd117214d4e93392cdae17bf117497501b05f7b2e25c6c8cb5cd43e1.png)\n- Two *vertices* are **adjacent** if there is a side joining them. For example, the vertex $E$ is adjacent to the vertices $D$ and $A$.![](images/6d/6d857920609da14fb0b31f762400c5f54e9e428717c2b55fcb17215280bbbfed.png)\n- A **diagonal** is a line segment that joins two non-adjacent vertices. For example, ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{AC}}}, {\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{BD}}}$, and ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{BE}}}$ are diagonals of our pentagon.\n\n![](images/ad/adf7afaa66144a33dba7ff3732b01619bc13cac0fae59c466393d4084c748a59.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "b82f1900-5686-5fcc-8354-6c00b102f576" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9676 :tutorial/title "The Parts of a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "0d4447dd-331d-5e2c-9e2b-470035c668cb" :step/math-academy-id 188125 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105756 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105492 :question/id #uuid "e72f5a3b-8085-54dc-a09a-121925ef509c" :question/math-academy-id "e-15617"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c58110-9813-5588-81d2-5735718589aa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106387}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Adjacent Vertices and Sides"} :step/id #uuid "a35aa384-e796-5e75-adfa-23d8efbf0c06" :step/math-academy-id 188126 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105757 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105493 :question/id #uuid "33bf6630-1558-50b9-8823-0bd58d0787af" :question/math-academy-id "e-15618"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed0dc2a-7c76-5fee-9aa8-aad529cf1952" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106389}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "94356407-759e-59ef-b5e4-6f11d01f5cf9" :step/math-academy-id 188129}] :activity/title "Polygons" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105971}]}
